## Reflection on the Enhancement Process

This enhancement gave me a clearer sense of the difference between a database enhancement and an application enhancement. All three pieces of this milestone — the index, the aggregation pipeline, and the schema validator — live entirely inside the database layer and the CRUD module that talks to it. The dashboard's presentation code only needed a small, additive change to display the new analytics panel; none of its existing filtering, table, or map behavior had to change.

The main challenge was verifying correctness without a live MongoDB server available to me while developing. I addressed this by installing **mongomock**, an in-memory MongoDB emulator, and writing a small automated test suite against it. I built a fixed sample dataset with hand-calculated expected results, ran `create_indexes()` and `get_breed_outcome_stats()` against that dataset, and asserted the returned counts, averages, and adoption rates matched what I'd calculated by hand. All four tests passed, giving me real confidence in the aggregation pipeline's correctness before ever running it against the actual Austin Animal Center collection.

> **A limitation I documented rather than hid:** the schema validator was the one piece I couldn't verify the same way, since mongomock does not enforce `collMod` validators. Rather than presenting the validator as tested when it wasn't, I documented that limitation directly.

I also learned to factor the rescue-type query-building logic — previously duplicated only in the table-update callback — into a single shared function, since the new analytics callback needed to filter on the exact same criteria. Duplicating that logic a second time would have let the two panels drift out of sync if the query criteria ever changed.
